#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "593eeb42-56b8-5177-8a9f-38d08a5186c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12584" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y$." :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= 3x^{2} - 6xy = 3x(x - 2y) \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x(x - 2y) & 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nSetting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x(x - 2y) = 0; 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y$. Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0$, we get\n$3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3$,\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n- For $x = 2y$, we obtain\n$3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1$.\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y =-1 & ⟹x = 2(-1) =-2 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟹x = 2(1) = 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$.\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$. Note that since\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nat these two points, they are also both stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "7c9f4b9e-be60-5da1-a140-08a3f183ce0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12585" :question/problem "The function\n$f(x,y) %= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(1,-1) > 0$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) < 0$\n3. The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 - 2x) =-2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 + 2y) = 2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} &= \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} = \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 + 2y) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{″}(1,-1) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)$, we get\n$H(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0$, the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "e5398577-d70c-56f9-9c89-4a9d4e8f1d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-12586" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function\n$f(x,y) % = x^3 -6xy + y^3$\ngiven that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf{a}$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\n(for stationary points) or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a})$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} - 3 = 0; 2y = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 1\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) = 6x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y) = 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0)$, we get\n$H(1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)) = 6 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = 12 > 0$.\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,0))_{11}= 6>0$, the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$.\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(-1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0)$, we get\n$H(-1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(-1,0)) = -6\\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -12 < 0$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99e0a30-00aa-5155-b32d-727997879c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122543} {:answer/id #uuid "b177cf4a-95c4-5b45-9ba0-dceacd3b1e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1, 1)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122544} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f0a83c-e7e0-5ac7-bcb1-c5a66e03b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122545} {:answer/id #uuid "dd337165-6c8a-531b-bf79-1db823e9a3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122546} {:answer/id #uuid "5deb9bf0-2dba-5540-90b1-a3d1d1cdbe2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a99e0a30-00aa-5155-b32d-727997879c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122543} :answer-field/id #uuid "362951f4-c185-5df4-ab93-d93165ccfa7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122542}] :question/id #uuid "cc5f47ca-79fc-5729-9bb1-01328bbbf467" :question/math-academy-id "q-36622" :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x, y) = 2\\ln x - 2xy + y^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999b3080-7512-5500-9d33-0e31df9f99a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123005} {:answer/id #uuid "9f681608-2729-5689-9204-d92823c3d1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123006} {:answer/id #uuid "0033cc0b-5c3b-5b57-b5b8-1ee22d75cebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123007} {:answer/id #uuid "103fb9e5-a535-5e67-8c1b-f5a0ad31c319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123008} {:answer/id #uuid "15fee360-f1b8-53c1-9662-b80ed83ecdba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "103fb9e5-a535-5e67-8c1b-f5a0ad31c319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123008} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4634b1d-d343-5b0f-b5ea-511294ead0b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123004}] :question/id #uuid "c3ea19ab-9bf6-5660-afe0-39f6942a18e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-37588" :question/problem "The function\n$f(x, y)$\nhas the first derivative\n$f^{′}(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand has a stationary point at $(0, 0)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(0, 0) > 0$\n2. $\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}}(0, 0) < 0$\n3. The point $(0, 0)$ is a saddle point of $f$"}]}
